basheur: everything eventually becomes bash. this is the negative signal. there are actually two streams. the usage stream and the dev stream. it is hard to tell them apart because at first they both have agent shape. over time, the usage stream should de-animate via the dev stream. at first, the agent call gives zach the result, one way or another, via the usage stream. but the usage stream leaves residue of the interaction. the dev stream's goal is to replace what the usage stream did agentically with a combination of linux enviroment spells, external calls, etc, with the aim that future usage calls use fewer tokens. when no usage calls require tokens, the development cycle completes. this means something like each project has a bash like contract. it takes in arguments flags etc. and promises an output. at first, an agent pretends to be the utility. but over time, the utility itself forms. this is a more sophisticated scope for realisateur's scaffolding. realisateur defines the contracts. basheur turns them into machine language over time. this means ideas properly route through realisateur. projects stop having *their own agent* so much. agents can be called from bash like summons when the tooling has not been built, yes, but agents also do the dev work. a more complex notion of self-dev where the dev team lives outside the project, somewhat. maybe the agent called to do the task always leaves a durable shell script behind (sedimentation, detritus) and the dev agents are tasked with wiring that in.
